# Hexagonal Architecture (Ports & Adapters)

Dependencies always point **inward**: adapters → application → domain. The core never knows about HTTP, gRPC, databases, or frameworks.

## Layout

```
cmd/<app>/main.go            # composition root: wires adapters into ports
internal/domain/             # entities, value objects, domain errors; pure Go, stdlib only
internal/application/        # use cases (services); depends only on domain + ports
internal/ports/              # interfaces: inbound (use cases) & outbound (repos, clock)
internal/adapters/inbound/   # HTTP/gRPC handlers -> call inbound ports
internal/adapters/outbound/  # repo implementations (memory, redis, postgres...)
```

## Rules

- `domain` imports nothing from the project except itself; no third-party libs.
- `application` imports only `domain` and `ports`; never an adapter package.
- Adapters implement ports; inbound adapters depend on inbound port interfaces, not concrete services.
- Define outbound port interfaces from the core's needs (small, consumer-side interfaces).
- DTOs/transport models live in adapters; map them to domain types at the boundary. Never leak JSON/DB tags into `domain`.
- Wiring (constructors, config, DI) happens only in `cmd/`.
- Pass `context.Context` as the first parameter of port methods that do I/O.

```go
// ❌ BAD: application depends on a concrete adapter
import "go-challenge/internal/adapters/outbound/redis"
type Service struct{ repo *redis.Repo }

// ✅ GOOD: application depends on a port
type Service struct {
	repo  ports.MembershipRepository
	clock ports.Clock
}
```

- When adding a feature: domain/port first (with tests), then application service, then adapter.
- Explain non-obvious architecture decisions in package doc comments (`doc.go`).
